--TEST--
A `#[Command]` method is `static` and returns `void` or `uint` — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 6's example writes both facts, and its dispatch requires the first: the
// compiled table holds a `Class::method` string and no constructor arguments
// anywhere in it, so there is no instance for a handler to be called on.
class Deploy {
    #[Core\Command(name: "deploy", about: "push the current build")]
    public function deploy(string $target): uint { return 0; }
}

// The second is `Core\Command::run(): uint` read backwards — a handler answers
// with the process exit status, and `void` is the status 0 it answers with when
// there is nothing to report.
class Release {
    #[Core\Command(name: "release", about: "ship it")]
    public static function release(string $target): string { return $target; }
}

// Both shapes § 6 admits, so the refusals above are about those two
// declarations rather than about the attribute.
class Build {
    #[Core\Command(name: "build", about: "compile everything")]
    public static function build(): uint { return 0; }

    #[Core\Command(name: "clean", about: "delete the artifacts")]
    public static function clean(): void {}
}

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0789]: the `#[Command]` method `deploy` is not `static`
  --> case.nvs:7:21
%A
error[E0789]: the `#[Command]` method `release` returns `string`
   --> case.nvs:15:28
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
